---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp/img/lab-1-14-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `let level = 18;

if (level < 25)
{
  fill(220, 60, 60);
}
else
{
  fill(80, 200, 120);
}

rect(50, 100, 300, 40);`;
const Figure2 = `let level = 140;
let barWidth = level;

if (barWidth > 300)
{
  barWidth = 300;
}

rect(50, 100, barWidth, 40);`;
const Figure3 = `let x = 260;
let y = 150;

if (x > 200)
{
  fill(220, 60, 60);
}
else
{
  fill(80, 120, 220);
}

ellipse(x, y, 40, 40);`;
const Task1 = `function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);

  let oxygen = 88;

  if (oxygen < 25)
  {
    fill(220, 60, 60);
  }
  else
  {
    fill(80, 200, 120);
  }

  rect(50, 60, oxygen * 3, 30);
}
`;
const Task2 = `function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);

  let oxygen = 88;
  let coolant = 18;

  if (oxygen < 25)
  {
    fill(220, 60, 60);
  }
  else
  {
    fill(80, 200, 120);
  }

  rect(50, 60, oxygen * 3, 30);

  if (coolant < 25)
  {
    fill(220, 60, 60);
  }
  else
  {
    fill(80, 200, 120);
  }

  rect(50, 110, coolant * 3, 30);
}
`;
const Task3 = `function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);

  let oxygen = 88;
  let coolant = 18;
  let power = 132;

  if (oxygen < 25)
  {
    fill(220, 60, 60);
  }
  else
  {
    fill(80, 200, 120);
  }

  rect(50, 60, oxygen * 3, 30);

  if (coolant < 25)
  {
    fill(220, 60, 60);
  }
  else
  {
    fill(80, 200, 120);
  }

  rect(50, 110, coolant * 3, 30);

  // Draw the power bar at y = 160, same pattern as the others
  //   work out its width from power, the same way
  //   if that width would be more than 300, clamp it to 300
  //   color it red under 25, yellow under 60, green otherwise
}
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.14: p5 Lab 3 &mdash; Decisions You Can See</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />


        <!-- What You're Building -->
        <section id="target">
          <h2>What You're Building</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image src={TargetImage} alt="A dark canvas with horizontal bars whose colors change according to their value" width={400} class="img-fluid rounded" />
          </div>

          <p>By the end of <b>Task 2</b> the bar colors will be decided by conditions rather than typed in.</p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Task 3 is not shown.</b> That one is yours to work out.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />
        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.14</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">p5 Lab 3 &mdash; Decisions You Can See</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Conditions That Choose a Color</p>
              <p>Clamping to a Boundary</p>
              <p>Reading a Picture as Data</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Nothing New Again</h2>
              <p>
                Conditionals from Activities 1.12 and 1.13. The canvas from
                1.7 and 1.10. No new syntax at all.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Nothing New Again</h2>
              <p>
                In the terminal, a wrong branch prints the wrong word and you
                have to be reading carefully to notice.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                On a canvas, a wrong branch is <b class="text-sn-theme">the
                wrong color, in front of you</b>. That is the entire reason for
                today.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Choosing a Color</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure1}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                The condition does not decide <i>whether</i> to draw. It decides{" "}
                <i>how</i>. The rectangle is drawn either way.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Where the Shape Goes Matters</h2>
              <p>
                The <CodeSnippet
                  code="rect()"
                  lang={"javascript"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> sits <b>after</b> the whole if/else, outside the braces.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <CodeSnippet
                  code="fill()"
                  lang={"javascript"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> sets a color that stays set. Put the shape inside one branch
                and it will only appear half the time &mdash; which is a
                different program.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Clamping</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure2}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Clamping</h2>
              <p>
                A bar 140 wide fits. A bar 400 wide runs off the canvas and looks
                broken.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                An <CodeSnippet
                  code="if"
                  lang={"javascript"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> with no <CodeSnippet
                  code="else"
                  lang={"javascript"}
                  theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                  inline
                /> is the right shape here: most of the time nothing needs to
                happen.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Notice the variable is <b>reassigned</b> &mdash; the same trick
                from Activity 1.5, now doing real work.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Boundaries in Space</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure3}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                A condition can test <i>position</i>. Anything past x = 200 turns
                red. This is how a program decides whether something is out of
                bounds, off-screen, or touching something else.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Using a condition to choose a fill color</li>
                <li>Clamping a value to a maximum</li>
                <li>Placing a shape outside the branches so it always draws</li>
                <li>Reading a drawing to check whether a branch is correct</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Clamp</dt>
                  <dd>
                    To limit a value so it cannot go past a maximum or minimum.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Boundary</dt>
                  <dd>
                    A limit that a condition tests against, such as the edge of
                    the canvas.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>State</dt>
                  <dd>
                    The current values a program is holding, which decide what it
                    draws.
                  </dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>p5.js</b> sketch named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">1-14-status-panel</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            You are building a status panel: one horizontal bar per system, its
            length showing the reading and its color showing whether that is a
            problem.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: One Bar</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it. Oxygen is at 88, so the bar should be green.</li>
            <li>
              Change <CodeSnippet
                code="oxygen"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> to 12 and run again. Same bar, shorter and red.
            </li>
            <li>Set it back to 88.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 3 — Decisions You Can See"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: A Second Bar</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a coolant reading at 18 and draw its bar 50 pixels below the
              first.
            </li>
            <li>
              Run it. You should see one green bar and one red bar &mdash; and
              you can tell which system is in trouble without reading a single
              number.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Note</b>: the green lines with a + sign
              are new code. Don't type the + sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 3 — Decisions You Can See"
            ins={{ range: "7, 20-29" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Plan, Then Add a Clamped Three-Color Bar</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The comments describe the power bar. It is harder than the other
              two in two ways.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>It needs clamping.</b> Power is 132, and 132 &times; 3 is 396,
              which runs off a 400-wide canvas. Limit the width to 300.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>It needs three colors, not two.</b> That means a chain, and the
              order matters &mdash; Activity 1.13's rule applies here exactly.
            </li>
            <li>
              Test it at 10, at 45, and at 132. You should get red, yellow, and
              green, and the last one should stop at the same place as a full bar.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 3 — Decisions You Can See"
            ins={{ range: "8, 32-35" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): A Warning Light</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a circle in the top-right corner that is red if <i>any</i> of
              the three systems is under 25, and green if none of them are.
            </li>
            <li>
              You do not have <CodeSnippet
                code="&&"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> or <CodeSnippet
                code="||"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> yet &mdash; those arrive in Activity 1.15. Solve it with the
              tools you have.
            </li>
            <li>
              There is more than one way. One approach uses a boolean variable
              that starts <CodeSnippet
                code="false"
                lang={"javascript"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              /> and gets reassigned.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your finished sketch should show all of the following.</p>

          <ul>
            <li>Three horizontal bars on a dark background, evenly spaced.</li>
            <li>The oxygen bar green and long; the coolant bar red and short.</li>
            <li>
              The power bar green and <b>stopping at 300 pixels</b>, not running
              off the edge.
            </li>
            <li>
              Every bar visible in every case &mdash; changing a reading should
              change a bar's length and color, never make it disappear.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>The test that matters:</b> set power to 10, 45, and 132 in turn.
            You should get three different colors. If two of them match, your
            chain is ordered wrong.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              What would happen if you moved a <code>rect()</code> call inside one
              branch of its if/else? Why is that a different program?
            </li>
            <li>
              The power bar needed clamping. Describe another situation where a
              program should limit a value rather than use it as given.
            </li>
            <li>
              You have now written chains in the terminal and on a canvas. Which
              made a wrongly-ordered chain easier to notice, and why?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
